Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 The H-CDCR Task L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Problem Formulation L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Evaluation Metrics L20:       1. cite10†Cluster-level Hierarchy Score L21:       2. cite11†Path-Distance Score L22:   4. cite12†3 SciCo Data Construction L23:     1. cite13†3.1 Documents, Mentions and Seed taxonomies L24:     2. cite14†3.2 Candidate Retrieval L25:     3. cite15†3.3 Data Annotation L26:     4. cite16†3.4 SciCo Properties L27:   5. cite17†4 Models L28:     1. cite18†4.1 Baseline Models L29:       1. cite19†Coreference Model L30:       2. cite20†Hierarchy Model L31:     2. cite21†4.2 Unified Model for H-CDCR L32:   6. cite22†5 Experimental Results L33:     1. cite23†5.1 Ablation Study L34:     2. cite24†5.2 Lexical Diversity Impact on Coreference L35:   7. cite25†6 Related Work L36:     1. cite26†Entity coreference resolution L37:     2. cite27†Entity linking L38:     3. cite28†Taxonomy construction L39:     4. cite29†Word sense induction L40:   8. cite30†7 Conclusion L41:     1. cite31†Acknowledgments: L42:   9. cite32†References L43:   10. cite33†A Data Collection L44:     1. cite34†A.1 Hypernym extraction details L45:     2. cite35†A.2 Mention Retrieval Details L46:     3. cite36†A.3 Annotation Interface L47:   11. cite37†B Models L48:     1. cite38†B.1 Cattan et al. [2021a]’s CDCR model L49:     2. cite39†B.2 Hyper parameters for the Unified Model L50:   12. cite40†C Coreference and Hierarchy Results L51: cite41†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L52: \nL53: arXiv:2104.08809v3 [cs.CL] 01 Sep 2021\nL54: # SciCo: Hierarchical Cross-Document Coreference\nL55: for Scientific Concepts\nL134: Curated list of lexically diverse candidates. Automatically collecting lexically diverse mentions is challenging; while our two automated approaches capture such examples, they also capture many lexically similar mentions, a common issue in coreference datasets (§cite24†5.2 ). To increase the diversity of SciCo and enable more transparency in evaluation (§cite24†5.2 ), we enrich SciCo with a manually-curated collection of 60 groups of closely related but lexically diverse concepts (400 in total).\nL135: For example, one group contains {deep learning, neural models, DNN algorithms}, another includes {class imbalance, skewed label distribution, imbalanced data problem}.\nL136: ### 3.2 Candidate Retrieval\nL137: We now turn to how we use the resources described in the previous section in order to select candidate mentions for annotation. Careful candidate generation is often necessary when collecting data for cross-document coreference cite54†Cybulska and Vossen (2014) ; cite66†Ravenscroft et al. (2021) , since the vast majority of random mention pairs are easy negatives. Following standard CDCR terminology, we refer to a batch of candidate mentions to be annotated together as a “topic”.\nL138: Given a topic, annotators are asked to form coreference clusters of mentions and label hierarchical relationships between clusters. Restricting the number of mentions in a topic is necessary in order to make the quadratic complexity of the task tractable.\nL139: We form topics of mentions (Figure cite68†3 (3)) as follows. First, we merge the PwC and hypernyms graphs (de-duplicating nodes) and form groups of candidate concepts where each group consists of a single parent concept and its children. We add our manually-curated groups to this set. We then form a topic from each group by matching each of its concepts against our large-scale corpus (§cite13†3.1 ).\nL140: Specifically, we retrieve the most similar mentions to each concept, in terms of cosine similarity of embeddings output by a neural model (details in Appendix cite35†A.2 ). The union of the retrieved mentions (and their contexts) for a given group form a single topic in our data set (Figure cite68†3 (4)). The mention retrieval step enriches SciCo with ambiguous cases (e.g., references to information extraction with very different meanings), as well as fine-grained variants of concepts.\nL179: To avoid creation of cyclical graphs, we adopt a greedy approach adding relations iteratively, starting from the highest entailment scores, discarding relations creating cycles. We apply this model to clusters obtained by each baseline.\nL180: ### 4.2 Unified Model for H-CDCR\nL181: Deciding whether two mentions refer to the same concept or have a hierarchical relation is sometimes non-trivial (e.g POS Tagging $\\xrightarrow{}$ PTB POS Tagging, but artificial neural networks and neural networks refer to the same concept).\nL182: Therefore, we develop a unified model by formulating our task as multiclass classification, where each mention pair $(m_{1},m_{2})$ can be assigned into four classes (0) $m_{1},m_{2}$ corefer (1) $m_{1}$ $\\xrightarrow{}$ $m_{2}$ (2) $m_{2}$ $\\xrightarrow{}$ $m_{1}$ or (3) not related. Consider a topic (pool of candidates,  §cite14†3.2 ), with a set of mentions $\\mathcal{M}$. During training, we learn a pairwise scorer $f(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ by minimizing:\nL183:  | $$L=-\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}m_{1},m_{2}\\in\\mathcal{M}\\\\\nL184: m_{1}\\neq m_{2}\\end{subarray}}{y\\cdot log(f(m_{1},m_{2}))}$$  |  | (2)\nL185: where $1$-hot $y$ is one of the four classes and $N$ is the number of training pairs. For $f(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ we use Longformer (cite86†Beltagy et al., 2020 ), a transformer-based language model for processing long sequences so that we can encode pairs of full paragraphs. We also use CDLM, a recent variant of Longformer pre-trained for cross-document tasks (cite47†Caciularu et al., 2021 ).\nL186: During pretraining, Longformer and CDLM apply local attention — attention only to tokens in a fixed-sized window around each token. When fine-tuning on a specific task, global attention — attention to all tokens in the sequence — can be assigned to a few target tokens to encode global information. Following cite47†Caciularu et al. (2021) , we take each mention and its corresponding paragraph, inserting mention markers <m> and </m> surrounding the mention to obtain a mention representation.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSciCo: Hierarchical Cross-Document Coreference for Scientific Concepts (https://arxiv.org/html/2104.08809)\nciteturn49view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn48view0\",\"pattern\":\"4.3\"}); Total lines: 388\nNo matching text found for \"4.3\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.18.pdf)\nciteturn49view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 21; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn48view1\",\"pattern\":\"3.1\"}); Total lines: 1442\nL0@P0: Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), pages 279–299\nL1@P0: August 11-16, 2024 ©2024 Association for Computational Linguistics\nL2@P0: SCIMON : Scientific Inspiration Machines Optimized for Novelty\nL3@P0: Qingyun Wang1, Doug Downey2, Heng Ji1, Tom Hope2,3\nL4@P0: 1 University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign 2 Allen Institute for Artificial Intelligence (AI2)\nL5@P0: 3 The Hebrew University of Jerusalem\nL6@P0: {tomh,doug}@allenai.org, {qingyun4,hengji}@illinois.edu\nL7@P0: Abstract\nL8@P0: We explore and enhance the ability of neu\u0002ral language models to generate novel scien\u0002tific directions grounded in literature. Work\nL9@P0: on literature-based hypothesis generation has\nL10@P0: traditionally focused on binary link prediction—\nL11@P0: severely limiting the expressivity of hypothe\u0002ses. This line of work also does not focus on\nL12@P0: optimizing novelty. We take a dramatic depar\u0002ture with a novel setting in which models use\nL13@P0: as input background contexts (e.g., problems,\nL301@P3: to create a gold subset. We manually exclude in\u0002stances with trivial overlap between ground truth\nL302@P3: and background, remove cases with irrelevant back\u0002ground, and retain only instances where the tar\u0002get relation (from which the seed term is taken)\nL303@P3: is salient to the target sentence. We also remove\nL304@P3: test pairs that have unexplained terms in the back\u0002ground. We obtain a total of 194 instances.4\nL305@P3: Split Forward Backward Total\nL306@P3: Train 55,884 58,426 114,310\nL307@P3: Valid 7,938 8,257 16,195\nL308@P3: Test 2,623 2,686 5,309\nL309@P3: Table 1: Dataset statistics. Considering a relation of the\nL310@P3: form [v used-for u], we define [v used-for ?] as\nL311@P3: forward, and [? used-for u] as backward.\nL312@P3: 3 SCIMON Models\nL313@P3: We present a new module to retrieve inspirations\nL314@P3: as contextual input (§3.1). Then, we describe\nL315@P3: another module to generate ideas given the con\u0002text+inspiration (§3.2). Finally, we introduce a\nL316@P3: new iterative novelty optimization method to fur\u0002ther improve idea quality (§3.3).5\nL317@P3: 3\nL318@P3: See Table 6 in Appendix.\nL319@P3: 4\nL320@P3: Full annotation details are in Appendix C.\nL321@P3: 5Training and hyperparameter details in Appendix B.\nL322@P3: 3.1 Inspiration Retrieval Module\nL323@P3: We take broad inspiration from cognitive aspects of\nL324@P3: innovation (Hope et al., 2023): when researchers\nL325@P3: generate a new idea, they are grounded in a web\nL326@P3: of existing concepts and papers bearing on the new\nL327@P3: idea. We aim to enrich the context of each back\u0002ground by retrieving “inspirations”— pieces of in\u0002formation that can guide hypothesis generation. As\nL328@P3: illustrated in Figure 2, for a given instance of the\nL329@P3: SCIMON task, our retrieval augmentation can re\u0002trieve from three types of sources. Each source\nL330@P3: uses a different form of query and output.\nL331@P3: Semantic Neighbors For a given prob\u0002lem/motivation as input, ideas proposed for related\nL332@P3: problems in the training set can serve as a guiding\nL333@P3: reference for generating a new idea. Given the\nL334@P3: background context B with a seed term v and\nL335@P3: problem/motivation M, we construct a base\nL336@P3: input b: a concatenation of M with a prompt P\nL337@P3: belonging to one of two templates: “v is used\nL338@P3: for p” or “v is done by using p”, where p is one\nL339@P3: of Task/Method/Material/Metric. In short, b :=\nL340@P3: P ⊕ context:M. For example, in Figure 2, the\nL341@P3: concatenation is “Knowledge acquisition is done\nL342@P3: by using Method; Context:...requires plms to\nL343@P3: integrate information...lifelong manner...”.\nL344@P3: We then retrieve inputs from the training set that\nL345@P3: are semantically related to a new base input b, and\nL346@P3: obtain target sentences T corresponding to each\nL347@P3: retrieved training input. We extract the target term\nL348@P3: t ∈ T matching the seed term in b (§2.2) as in\u0002spiration for input b. Simply put, this means we\nL349@P3: use as inspiration the salient aspect of the solution\nL350@P3: proposed in T , which we found empirically to help\nL351@P3: remove noisy/irrelevant information in T . For ex\u0002ample, in Figure 2, we find “informative entities\nL352@P3: are done by using Method context: in this work, we\nL353@P3: aim at equipping pre-trained language models with\nL354@P3: structured knowledge.” as similar to the input and\nL355@P3: use t =“linked knowledge graph” as inspiration.\nL356@P3: Technically, we first construct a fully connected\nL357@P3: graph GS based on the training set where each node\nL358@P3: is a pair of input text bi and target term ti. We\nL359@P3: define the weight between two nodes i and j as\nL360@P3: the cosine similarity between bi and bj based on\nL361@P3: representations from SentenceBERT (Reimers and\nL362@P3: Gurevych, 2019) (all-mpnet-base-v2). Given b,\nL363@P3: we first insert it into GS and compute the weights\nL1084@P12: best in semantic search to retrieve similar nodes\nL1085@P12: from the training set based on query q in §3.1. We\nL1086@P12: retrieve up to 20 relevant semantic neighbors R\nL1087@P12: from the training set for each instance. We treat the\nL1088@P12: target nodes from R as semantic neighbors.\nL1089@P12: B.1.2 KG Neighbors\nL1090@P12: We use one-hop connected neighbors from the\nL1091@P12: background KG GB constructed on papers before\nL1092@P12: 2021(i.e., the papers in the training set). Because\nL1093@P12: of the scarcity of KG neighbors, we do not limit\nL1094@P12: the number of KG neighbors.\nL1095@P12: B.1.3 Citation Neighbors\nL1096@P12: Similar to semantic neighbors, we use\nL1097@P12: all − mpnet − base − v2 from Sentence\u0002Bert (Reimers and Gurevych, 2019) to retrieve\nL1098@P12: cited paper titles similar to query q. We restrict\nL1099@P12: cited papers only before 2021. We retrieve up\nL1100@P12: to 5 relevant citation neighbors from the papers’\nL1101@P12: citation network.\nL1102@P12: B.2 Generation Module\nL1103@P12: Our T5 model and their variants are built based on\nL1104@P12: the Huggingface framework (Wolf et al., 2020).16\nL1105@P12: We optimize those models by AdamW (Loshchilov\nL1106@P12: and Hutter, 2019) with the linear warmup sched\u0002uler.17 Those models are finetuned on 4 NVIDIA\nL1107@P12: A6000 48GB GPUs with distributed data parallel.18\nL1108@P12: The training time for each model is about 10 hours.\nL1109@P12: 15The similarity is calculated with all-mpnet-base-v2.\nL1110@P12: 16github.com/huggingface/transformers\nL1111@P12: 17huggingface.co/docs/transformers/main_classes\nL1112@P12: /optimizer_schedules#transformers.get_linear_sch\nL1113@P12: edule_with_warmup\nL1114@P12: 18pytorch.org/tutorials/intermediate/ddp_tutor\nL1115@P12: ial.html\nL1116@P12-13: 291Used-for\nL1117@P13: Used-for\nL1118@P13: Feature-of\nL1119@P13: Used-for\nL1120@P13: Used-for\nL1121@P13: Coref\nL1122@P13: Method Task Other Scientific Terms\nL1123@P13: Impressive milestones have been achieved in text matching by adopting a cross-attention mechanism to capture pertinent\nL1124@P13: semantic connections between two sentence representations.\nL1125@P13: However, regular cross-attention focuses on word-level links between the two input sequences, neglecting the importance\nL1126@P13: of contextual information.\nL1127@P13: We propose a context-aware interaction network (COIN) to properly align two sequences and infer their semantic relationship.\nL1128@P13: Background Sentence\nL1129@P13: Context-Aware Interaction Network for Question Matching\nL1130@P13: Figure 4: Preprocessing result for Hu et al. (2021) in non-canonicalized KG Corpus\nL1131@P13: Stage PL-Maker\nL1132@P13: Entities\nL1133@P13: PL-Maker\nL1134@P13: Used-for Relations\nL1135@P13: SciCo Coreference Scispacy Abbreviation\nL1136@P13: Detection\nL1137@P13: Sentence\nL1138@P13: Classification\nL1139@P13: Precision 91.3 65.4 97.2 100 100\nL1140@P13: Table 6: Human quality evaluation of preprocessing stages(%). Overall pass rate after all steps are applied is 79.7%.\nL1141@P13: Type Train Valid Test\nL1142@P13: SN 10.8 10.0 10.0\nL1143@P13: KG 8.3 8.0 8.1\nL1144@P13: CT 4.9 5.0 5.0\nL1145@P13: Table 7: Average of # of neighbors for each instance,\nL1146@P13: excluding those which do not have any neighbor\nL1147@P13: B.2.1 In-Context Learning\nL1148@P13: We choose GPT3.5 davinci-00319 (Brown et al.,\nL1149@P13: 2020) as our out-of-the-box causal language mod\u0002eling baseline. We select 5 instances from the train\u0002ing set as examples for the few-shot setting. We\nL1150@P13: randomly select those examples for GPT3.5FS. For\nL1151@P13: GPT3.5Retr, similar to semantic neighbors, we use\nL1152@P13: all-mpnet-base-v2 from SentenceBert (Reimers\nL1153@P13: and Gurevych, 2019), which performs best in se\u0002mantic search to retrieve similar instances from the\nL1154@P13: training set based on query q in §3.1. The input\nL1155@P13: length is limited to 2048 tokens due to OpenAI\nL1156@P13: API limits. We choose gpt-4-0314 as our GPT4\nL1157@P13: model. Our input for GPT4 is similar to GPT3.5.\nL1158@P13: For each selected example from the training set\nL1159@P13: with forward relation, the template is “Consider\nL1160@P13: the following context: M In that context, which p\nL1161@P13: can be used for v, and why? T ”, where M is the\nL1162@P13: background context, p is the target node type, v is\nL1163@P13: the seed term, and T is the target idea sentence; for\nL1164@P13: backward relation, the template is “Consider the\nL1165@P13: following context: M In that context, which p do\nL1166@P13: we use v, and why? s”. For selected examples with\nL1167@P13: 19openai.com/api/\nL1168@P13: additional retrieval inspirations, we concatenate\nL1169@P13: the following additional template to the M: “The\nL1170@P13: retrieval results are: i1, . . . , ik”, where i1, . . . , ik\nL1171@P13: are retrieved inspirations. For the final prompt, the\nL1172@P13: template is similar to the above example template.\nL1173@P13: However, the target sentence T will not be included.\nL1174@P13: We ask the model to generate 10 outputs. We will\nL1175@P13: select the best output and skip the empty output.\nL1176@P13: B.2.2 Fine Tuning\nL1177@P13: Given input without any inspirations, the input com\u0002bines the prompt P and context M as shown in\nL1178@P13: §3.1 (i.e., P | context: M). Given input with in\u0002spirations, the input is P | retrieve: i1, . . . , ik |\nL1179@P13: context: M, with i1, . . . , ik as retrieved inspira\u0002tions. The input length is limited to 512 tokens.\nL1180@P13: For both tasks, we finetune our model based on\nL1181@P13: T5-large with a learning rate of 6 × 10−6and\nL1182@P13: ϵ = 1 × 10−6. The batch size is 8 for each GPU.\nL1183@P13: The maximum training epoch for all models is 10\nL1184@P13: with 4 patience. During decoding, we use beam\u0002search to generate results with a beam size of 5 and\nL1185@P13: a repetition penalty of 1.5.\nL1186@P13: In-context Contrastive Augmentation We ran\u0002domly select 2 sentences that appeared in the in\u0002put as in-context negatives. For example, in Fig\u0002ure 1, the in-context negatives could be “knowl\u0002edge acquisition is done by using Method”, “this\nL1187@P13: requires plms to integrate the information from all\nL1188@P13: the sources in a lifelong manner .”.\nL1189@P13-14: 292B.2.3 Biochemical Case Study\nL1190@P14: Our Meditron-7b (Chen et al., 2023) and its\nL1191@P14: variants are built based on the Huggingface\nL1192@P14: framework (Wolf et al., 2020).20 We use its\nL1193@P14: epfl-llm/meditron-7b as the base model. We\nL1194@P14: finetune those models with a learning rate of\nL1195@P14: 2 × 10−6and ϵ = 5 × 10−8. The maximum train\u0002ing epoch for all models is 5. All models are fine\u0002tuned on 4 NVIDIA A100 80 GB GPUs with Fully\nL1196@P14: Sharded Data Parallel.21 The training time for each\nL1197@P14: model is about 20 hours.\nL1198@P14: B.3 The Scale of Retrieval Set\nL1199@P14: We retrieve from a set of 59k papers with over\nL1200@P14: 374k sentences in the NLP domain, the focus of\nL1201@P14: our experiments. Our background KG built on the\nL1202@P14: training set has more than 197k nodes and 261k re\u0002lations. Moreover, we collect 87k paper titles from\nL1203@P14: citation networks. This represents a large-scale and\nL1204@P14: diverse domain; retrieving inspirations from this set\nL1205@P14: is expected, in principle, to be more than enough\nL1206@P14: for generating novel ideas. Indeed, NLP papers\nL1207@P14: typically cite each other and build on each other as\nL1427@P17: only bi-functional amino acid decarboxylase in Saccharomyces cerevisiae.\nL1428@P17: Table 12: Input and idea-sentence generation results for seed gene “ARO10” in the biochemical domain.\nL1429@P17: Annotator Pair 1-2 1-3 1-4 1-5 1-6\nL1430@P17: Agreement % 68.8 75.0 56.2 43.8 75.0\nL1431@P17: Table 13: Percent (%) of same labels from overlapped\nL1432@P17: 10 human evaluation instances on each pair of annota\u0002tors for Study I.\nL1433@P17: Annotator Pair 1-2 1-3 1-4 1-5\nL1434@P17: Agreement % 92.5 93.3 87.5 90.0\nL1435@P17: Table 14: Percent (%) of same labels from overlapped\nL1436@P17: 20 human evaluation instances on each pair of annota\u0002tors for Study III. (1-3) has 60 shared questions. The\nL1437@P17: rest of the pairs each share 40 questions.\nL1438@P17-18: 296Figure 6: Human evaluation instructions\nL1439@P18-19: 297Figure 7: Human evaluation example for GPT3.5Rnd, GPT3.5Retr, GPT3.5Rnd+CT, T5, and T5+SN+CL\nL1440@P19-20: 298Figure 8: Human evaluation example for GPT3.5Rnd+KG, GPT4Rnd, and GPT4Rnd+KG\nL1441@P20: 299\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSOLVENT (https://www.hyadatalab.com/papers/analogy-cscw18.pdf)\nciteturn49view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 21; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn48view2\",\"pattern\":\"4.2\"}); Total lines: 904\nL332@P7: We begin our investigation of solvent’s quality and feasibility by sourcing and using annotations\nL333@P7: from domain expert researchers. This deployment should provide an upper bound on solvent’s\nL334@P7: quality and cost considerations.\nL335@P7: 4.1 Dataset\nL336@P7: As an initial test, we deployed our modified mixed-initative system on 50 papers published at the\nL337@P7: CSCW conference4from 2013-2017. This modest number of papers is small enough for experts to\nL338@P7: manually find analogies (with reasonable confidence that we have covered most analogies), yet\nL339@P7: large enough to produce interpretable quantitative results (N=1,225 possible pairs between papers).\nL340@P7: Further, the match to our content/domain expertise (social computing) allows us to better define\nL341@P7: what counts as analogies, and judge the performance of the different metrics accordingly.\nL342@P7: 4.2 Annotation and Vectors\nL343@P7: Two members of the research team served as annotators. Each paper abstract took a median of\nL344@P7: 1 minute to annotate. In Studies 2 and 3, we explore how the method extends to settings where\nL345@P7: annotators lack content/domain expertise.\nL346@P7: 4http://cscw.acm.org/\nL347@P7-8: Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction, Vol. 2, No. CSCW, Article 31. Publication date: November 2018.Mixed Initiative System for Finding Analogies 31:9\nL348@P8: Match Type Title & Explanation\nL349@P8: Near analogy: Background +\nL350@P8: Purpose + Mechanism\nL351@P8: Toward Collaborative Ideation at Scale: Leveraging Ideas from\nL352@P8: Others to Generate More Creative and Diverse Ideas: Use crowd\u0002sourcing to identify diverse idea sets to improve creative idea generation\nL353@P8: Far analogy: Purpose + Mecha\u0002nismCrowd Guilds: Worker-led Reputation and Feedback on Crowd\u0002sourcing Platforms: Make worker certification self-sustainable by using\nL354@P8: crowdsourced peer review to certify good workers\nL355@P8: Non-analogy Making Decisions From a Distance: The Impact of Technological\nL356@P8: Mediation on Riskiness and Dehumanization: No similarity\nL536@P12: 5.4.1 Our metrics find more analogies than all words baseline. Overall, the researcher identified\nL537@P12: 7 out of the 53 matches as relevant and new. This reflects the extremely challenging setting of\nL538@P12: finding useful and novel analogies among 90 randomly sampled papers from outside the core\nL539@P12: domain of the work. 5 out of those 7 analogies were found by one of/both the purpose+mechanism\nL540@P12: and mechanism similarity metrics (an aggregate precision of approximately .25; comparable to\nL541@P12: precision at K=.2 in Study 1), while 2 were found by the all words metric (precision = .1).\nL542@P12: 5.4.2 Our metrics find different analogies from all words baseline. In addition to this numerical\nL543@P12: difference, there was also a qualitative difference in the types of matches returned. To illustrate,\nL544@P12: the most relevant match found by the all words metric was almost a direct replication of their\nL545@P12: work (manipulating the structure of a membrane in a controlled way by applying tension to the\nL546@P12: membrane) from an aerospace engineering paper. The researcher was uncertain if she had seen\nL547@P12: this paper before, and found it useful for competitive analysis.\nL548@P12: In contrast, the most relevant match found by the purpose+mechanism metric was a paper on\nL549@P12: using multi-agent systems to generate a variety of geometric structures. The mechanism metric\nL550@P12: also found an interesting potential analogy of a civil engineering paper analyzing web crippling\nL551@P12: phenomena in steel beams under tension. Both of these matches were judged to be both potentially\nL552@P12: useful (the first for inspiring alternative approaches to systematically explore 2D/3D structures by\nL553@P12: applying different levels and types of tension, the second for potentially inspiring new mathematical\nL554@P12: models to analyze their polymers, which could reveal new properties to leverage for their designs)\nL555@P12: and novel (neither had been seen by the researcher).\nL556@P12: 6 STUDY 3: SCALING UP SOLVENT THROUGH CROWDSOURCING\n"
